	<article class="hand-card" style="margin: 4rem auto; max-width: 800px; padding: 3rem;">
		<h2 class="font-heading" style="font-size: 2.5rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; color: var(--fg-pencil);">Planning a Full Open Bar: The Ultimate Guide</h2>
		
		<div style="text-align: center; margin: 2rem 0;">
			<img src="/images/wedding_bar_sketch.webp" alt="Wedding Liquor Calculator — Open Bar Bottle Estimator" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="max-width: 100%; border: 3px solid var(--fg-pencil); border-radius: 2px; box-shadow: 4px 4px 0px 0px rgba(45, 45, 45, 0.15); transform: rotate(1deg);" />
		</div>

		<p class="font-body" style="font-size: 1.2rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
			Figuring out exactly how much alcohol to buy for a wedding or large event can be incredibly stressful. No one wants the bar to run dry halfway through the reception, but overbuying by hundreds of dollars isn't fun either. That's why we built this interactive <strong>Event Alcohol Calculator</strong>—to give you a precise, stress-free shopping list. (Be sure to check out the real-time dynamic graph that adjusts as you move the sliders!)
		</p>
		<p class="font-body" style="font-size: 1.2rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
			Whether you're stocking up for a massive wedding reception, a corporate holiday party, or a backyard blowout, the math remains largely the same. Our tool takes the guesswork out of the process, letting you adjust exactly what your crowd actually likes to drink.
		</p>

		<h3 class="font-heading" style="font-size: 2rem; margin-top: 2rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; color: var(--accent-red);">Why Use a Custom Drink Calculator?</h3>
		<p class="font-body" style="font-size: 1.2rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
			A lot of basic wedding alcohol calculators just spit out a generic number of "bottles." But if you're serving two signature cocktails that require tequila and vodka, a generic liquor estimate won't help your bartenders at all. By tweaking the interactive sliders above, you get a custom breakdown of every specific spirit, mixer, and garnish you'll need.
		</p>
		<p class="font-body" style="font-size: 1.2rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
			For example, if you know your family prefers whiskey over wine, just slide the liquor preference up and bump the whiskey slider to match. You'll instantly see your shopping list update with the exact number of 750ml bottles required, preventing annoying shortages mid-party.
		</p>

		<h3 class="font-heading" style="font-size: 2rem; margin-top: 2rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; color: var(--accent-blue);">The Golden Rule of Event Bartending</h3>
		<p class="font-body" style="font-size: 1.2rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
			The standard industry rule of thumb is remarkably simple: assume <strong>one drink per guest, per hour</strong>. While Uncle Bob might crush four beers in an hour, Aunt Sally might nurse a single glass of wine all night. Across a large crowd, it averages out perfectly.
		</p>
		<p class="font-body" style="font-size: 1.2rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
			If you're offering a full open bar, the classic breakdown is roughly 40% wine, 40% liquor, and 20% beer. However, younger crowds tend to skew heavily toward spirits and cocktails, while afternoon or outdoor events might lean heavily toward beer and seltzers. Trust your gut and adjust the sliders to match your specific guest list!
		</p>

		<h3 class="font-heading" style="font-size: 2rem; margin-top: 2rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; color: var(--accent-yellow);">Frequently Asked Questions</h3>
		<details style="margin-bottom: 1rem; cursor: pointer; padding: 1rem; border: 2px dashed var(--fg-pencil); border-radius: 4px;">
			<summary class="font-heading" style="font-size: 1.2rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; outline: none;">Do I need to buy exactly what the calculator says?</summary>
			<p class="font-body" style="margin-top: 0.5rem; margin-bottom: 0;">No! The calculator is a baseline. If you know your family prefers whiskey over vodka, adjust the sliders. It's always a good idea to add a buffer to your final shopping list.</p>
		</details>
		<details style="margin-bottom: 1rem; cursor: pointer; padding: 1rem; border: 2px dashed var(--fg-pencil); border-radius: 4px;">
			<summary class="font-heading" style="font-size: 1.2rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; outline: none;">How much ice do I really need?</summary>
			<p class="font-body" style="margin-top: 0.5rem; margin-bottom: 0;">For a full open bar serving mixed drinks, 2 lbs of ice per guest is recommended. Cocktails require ice for both mixing/shaking and serving in the glass.</p>
		</details>
		<details style="margin-bottom: 1rem; cursor: pointer; padding: 1rem; border: 2px dashed var(--fg-pencil); border-radius: 4px;">
			<summary class="font-heading" style="font-size: 1.2rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; outline: none;">Can I return unopened alcohol?</summary>
			<p class="font-body" style="margin-top: 0.5rem; margin-bottom: 0;">This depends entirely on your state laws and the retailer. Many large liquor stores allow you to return unopened bottles with labels intact, making it safer to over-buy.</p>
		</details>

